    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "What the sampled vacancies show"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "The dated search identified employer and third-party listings linked to Kinshasa and other DRC locations. Several entries concern donor-funded or proposal-contingent projects and must not be counted as unconditional hires. Others require specialized qualifications or advertise expatriate recruitment. A vacancy is a demand signal, not evidence that a person was hired."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "[R12] [R13] [R15] [R16] [R18]"
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "What cannot yet be concluded"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "The sample cannot establish the total number of national technology openings, the most common career nationally, vacancy fill rates or the number of qualified Congolese applicants. Large employers also recruit through closed networks, contractor pipelines and project partners. Missing postings might reflect incomplete capture rather than missing jobs. Equally, a single specialist advert does not establish a mature local employment ecosystem."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "[R22] [R23] [R24]"
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "A reproducible audit protocol"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Collect listings weekly from employer sites, national job boards and verified program vacancies. Deduplicate by employer, title, location and URL. Classify industry, technical skill, salary disclosure, residency requirements, local or expatriate preference, funding contingency and closing date. Record both newly opened and expired postings to distinguish persistent demand from turnover."
    },
